{"name":"Blue Magpie","hex":"#9ac0de","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"magpie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of several kinds of bird in the family Corvidae, especially Pica pica.","A superficially similar Australian bird, Gymnorhina tibicen, in the family Artamidae."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mark with patches of black and white or light and dark.","To steal or hoard (items) as magpies are believed to do."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/magpie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Magpie (ship)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Magpie was a 105-meter Japanese freighter owned by Jinyu Shipping Company. She was registered at Panama, and chartered to a Dutch corporation. In November 1983, she ran aground at Newport, Oregon, spilling 265,000 litres of fuel oil into the coastal waters."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Magpie_(ship)","wikidata":"Q4929394"},{"title":"Red-billed blue magpie","blocks":[{"text":"The red-billed blue magpie is a species of bird (magpie) in the crow family, Corvidae. It is about the same size as the Eurasian magpie, but has a much longer tail, one of the longest of any corvid. It is 65–68 cm (25.5–27 in) long and weighs 196–232 g (6.9–8.2 oz)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Urocissa_erythrorhyncha.jpg/330px-Urocissa_erythrorhyncha.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red-billed_blue_magpie","wikidata":"Q766306"},{"title":"Urocissa","blocks":[{"text":"Urocissa is a genus of birds in the Corvidae, a family that contains the crows, jays, and magpies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Urocissa_caerulea.jpg/330px-Urocissa_caerulea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urocissa","wikidata":"Q904303"},{"title":"Magpie","blocks":[{"text":"True magpies are birds of various species of the family Corvidae. Like other members of their family, they are widely considered to be intelligent creatures. The Eurasian magpie, for instance, is thought to rank among the world's most intelligent creatures, and is one of the few nonmammalian species able to recognize itself in a mirror test. Magpies have shown the ability to make and use tools, imitate human speech, grieve, play games, and work in teams. They are particularly well known for their songs and were once popular as cagebirds. In addition to other members of the genus Pica, corvids considered magpies are in the genera Cissa, Urocissa, and Cyanopica."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Pica_pica_-_Compans_Caffarelli_-_2012-03-16.jpg/330px-Pica_pica_-_Compans_Caffarelli_-_2012-03-16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magpie","wikidata":"Q2495957"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Red-billed_Blue_Magpie.JPG/500px-Red-billed_Blue_Magpie.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Red-billed_Blue_Magpie.JPG/500px-Red-billed_Blue_Magpie.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Red-billed blue magpie","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed-billed_Blue_Magpie.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Red-billed_Blue_